@!internal

module nupp.io

--[[
`io` is bytes: storage for them, and the readers and writers that move
them. Reach for it when a program needs a growable buffer, an immutable
snapshot of one, or a parser that works the same over a buffer, a file and an
HTTP response body.

```nupp
local bytes = io.newbuffer("hello")
local reader = bytes:newreader()
assert(reader:read(5) == "hello")
```

A buffer holds its bytes in native-layout storage the VM manages rather than in
a Lua string. `Reader` and `Writer` are
interfaces rather than the concrete things that satisfy them. Code written
against either contract works over every implementation without knowing which
one it has.

The rest of what `io` means is a module of its own, because each has a luacase
name and so can have one. See [](io) for filesystem names,
[](io) for resource identifiers, [](io.files) for the filesystem
itself, `popen` for running a child process, [](io.net) for
plain and encrypted network connections, and [](io.http)
for the asynchronous HTTP client.
Keeping them apart is what lets a program that wants a byte buffer carry a byte
buffer rather than every provider behind the name.

Files, processes, network connections, TLS sessions, and HTTP bodies move bytes
through the `Reader` and `Writer` contracts on this page. A parser written
against byte I/O therefore accepts all of them.

Buffers, views, readers, writers, and their adapters are owners. A factory that
takes a reader or writer transfers its close obligation to the adapter; a
factory that borrows a buffer or byte queue leaves that value with its caller.
`close` consumes an owner. Types that expose `isreleased` report their state
without changing it.

## Buffers

`newbuffer()` creates an empty growable buffer. A string supplies initial
bytes; an integer reserves capacity without changing the length.

```nupp
local bytes = io.newbuffer("hello")
bytes:setstring("!", bytes:len())
assert(bytes:getstring() == "hello!")
```

Buffer offsets are zero-based, as they are wherever a count is an offset into
storage rather than a position in a Lua string. See [Byte
positions](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md#byte-positions) for the
rule and where the other convention applies.

`getstring(offset, count)` copies a range. `setstring` overwrites from an
offset and grows the buffer as needed, filling any gap with zero bytes. `clear`
sets the length to zero without discarding capacity, and `resize` truncates or
zero-fills:

```nupp
local bytes = io.newbuffer("hello")
bytes:ensure(4096)
assert(bytes:capacity() >= 4096)
bytes:resize(3)
assert(bytes:getstring() == "hel")
```

Capacity is the allocation, not a recorded number. Growing at least doubles it,
so appending through a writer costs amortized constant time per byte and
`capacity()` reports bytes that are actually held. `ensure` reserves at
least the minimum asked for.

`close()` releases the buffer's storage and returns nothing, and an operation on
a released buffer raises rather than answering a reason.

`readspan()` borrows the buffer's initialized bytes without separating its
pointer from its length. Native producers write through `reservewrite(offset,
count)`: the returned lease supplies a checked writable span, and
`commit(written)` changes the logical length only after the producer succeeds.
Dropping the lease aborts it and leaves the length unchanged.

## Byte views

`view(offset, count)` returns an immutable snapshot, not a mutable alias into
the buffer. It remains valid if the source buffer changes or closes:

```nupp
local buffer = io.newbuffer("header-body")
local header = buffer:view(0, 6)
buffer:clear()
assert(header:getstring() == "header")
header:close()
```

A view can make a smaller view, open its own snapshot reader, report its byte
length, copy to a string, borrow a checked span with `readspan()`, and be
closed. Its offsets are zero-based like a buffer's. The data and UTF-8 modules
accept views, which is what keeps those APIs from being coupled to a mutable
buffer.

## Readers

A `io.Reader` is a forward-only byte source. `newreader(text)` reads
a string; `buffer:newreader()` reads a snapshot of the buffer's current
contents. A reader owns its close obligation and closes automatically at the
end of its scope unless `close()` consumes it earlier.

```nupp
local reader = io.newreader("abcdef")
assert(reader:read(2) == "ab")

local destination = io.newbuffer()
assert(reader:readinto(destination, 0, 3) == 3)
assert(destination:getstring() == "cde")
assert(reader:read(8) == "f")
assert(reader:read(8) == "") -- EOF
```

`read(count)` requires a positive count and returns at most that many bytes. It
returns an empty string at EOF and nil with a reason after close.
`readspan(writable)` fills a positive-sized checked writable span.
`readinto(buffer, offset, count)` returns zero at EOF and reads 64 KiB when
given no count; an explicit count must be positive. A failed or EOF read does
not extend the destination. `transferto(writer)` copies the entire remaining
source and returns the byte count.

A read moves the reader's cursor as a write moves a writer's, so all four take
the reader exclusively. Two readers over one cursor, such as a stream and a
borrowed view of it, cannot both be reading at once; the checker refuses the
second while the first is live.

`readall(reader, maxbytes)` takes a reader and returns everything left in it as
one string, closing the reader on the way out. A limit is checked as the bytes
arrive, so a source larger than it returns a reason rather than a string:

```nupp
assert(io.readall(io.newreader("whole")) == "whole")
local text, reason = io.readall(io.newreader("too long"), 3)
assert(text == nil and reason ~= nil)
```

## Writers

`buffer:newwriter()` clears the buffer and returns a forward-only writer
targeting it. A writer likewise closes automatically; `flush()` publishes
pending work without consuming that ownership. The writer holds its buffer
exclusively for as long as it is live, so the buffer is read once the writer is
gone:

```nupp
local destination = io.newbuffer()
do
    local writer = destination:newwriter()
    assert(writer:write("prefix:"))

    local payload = io.newbuffer("body")
    assert(writer:writespan(payload:readspan()) == 4)
    assert(writer:flush())
end
assert(destination:getstring() == "prefix:body")
```

`write` returns a boolean and `writespan` returns the byte count. Both return a
reason when the writer is closed or the destination was released. The span is
the common zero-copy input contract for buffers, views, files, processes, and
HTTP transfers; there is no unchecked raw-pointer writer alongside it. `flush`
does nothing for memory. It is part of the contract because a file or socket
buffers, and `flush` is where one reports a write that failed to land: `close`
returns nothing, so a caller that has to know flushes first.

## Lines

`Reader:lines(limit)` takes its reader and separates it at `\n` and `\r\n`.
The terminator is not returned, and a final fragment before EOF is still a
line:

```nupp
local lines = io.newreader("one\r\ntwo\nlast"):lines()
assert(lines:read() == "one")
assert(lines:read() == "two")
assert(lines:read() == "last")
assert(lines:read() == nil) -- EOF
```

The default limit is 64 KiB. An overlong line or source failure returns nil
with a reason. Closing or dropping the line reader closes the source it took.

## Typed scalars

A reader and a writer move bytes. `newsreader` and `newswriter` add
the missing piece, a sized integer or float landing on those bytes without
decoding it by hand at every call site.

```nupp
local writer = io.newswriter()
writer:writeuint32(1447383632):writefloat64(1.5)

local stored = writer:buffer()
assert(stored ~= nil)
local reader = io.newsreader(stored)
assert(reader:readuint32() == 1447383632)
assert(reader:readfloat64() == 1.5)
assert(reader:atend())
```

Every write returns the writer, so calls chain. A short read raises rather than
answering a reason, which is the one place this pair departs from the reader and
writer contracts above: a scalar either landed whole or the source was not what
the format said, and there is no partial value to hand back.

### Scalar sources

`newsreader` takes whatever holds the bytes. A string, a `ByteView` or a
`Buffer` is read from a copy taken there and then, so `remaining()` knows the
count:

```nupp
local reader = io.newsreader("\1\2\3\4")
assert(reader:remaining() == 4)
```

A `Reader` is consumed as it goes, which is what lets a file or an HTTP body be
read a field at a time. It cannot say how much is left, so `remaining()` returns
nil and `atend()` is the question to ask instead. The scalar adapter takes that
reader: closing the adapter, by hand or at the end of its scope, closes the
reader exactly once.

### Scalar destinations

`newswriter` appends to a `Buffer`, keeping what that buffer already
holds, or writes through a `Writer`. Given nothing it starts a buffer of its
own, which `buffer()` hands back:

```nupp
local destination = io.newbuffer("header:")
do
    local scalars = io.newswriter(destination)
    scalars:writeuint8(33)
end
assert(destination:getstring() == "header:!")
```

A writer pointed at somebody else's `Writer` returns nil from `buffer()`, since
the bytes are already gone. That writer is taken, so closing the scalar adapter
closes it exactly once. A buffer supplied by the caller is only borrowed. The
buffer created by the no-argument form belongs to the scalar writer; `buffer()`
borrows it for as long as the scalar writer remains live.

## Failures

The files, process, network, TLS and HTTP modules return a failure from the
environment as `nil, err`, where `err` is a [](io.Error): a `kind` a
program can branch on and a `message` a person can read. The kind is the same on
every platform and provider, so a missing file is `"notfound"` whether the
operating system or the browser said so; the message is whatever the platform
said and is not.

```nupp
local text, err = io.files.read("settings.json")
if text == nil and err ~= nil and err.kind == "notfound" then
    text = "{}"
end
```

An error prints as its message, and joins text the same way, so
`assert(files.read(path))` and `print("cannot read: " .. err)` read as they
would with a string. The reads and writes of the `Reader` and `Writer` contracts
are the exception: their reason stays a string, the same message, because a
buffer or a string reader returns through the same contract.

## Byte queues

A [`nupp.text`](nupp.text) buffer is a byte queue: `put` appends to the back
and `get` consumes from the front. `newsreader` accepts one directly, so
bytes assembled there are read without copying everything in it first, and the
queue's own `get` stays usable over the same bytes.

```nupp
local queue = nupp.text.newbuffer()
queue:put("header:")
queue:put("!")

local reader = io.newsreader(queue)
assert(reader:readbytes(7) == "header:")
assert(queue:tostring() == "!")
assert(reader:readuint8() == 33)
```

Reading drains the queue, and `remaining()` reports what it still holds.
`newreader` is the same bridge one layer down: it returns an ordinary
`Reader` over the queue, for `transferto`, `readinto`, and everything else that
contract already covers.

```nupp
local destination = io.newbuffer()
local queue = nupp.text.newbuffer()
queue:put("payload")
do
    local reader = io.newreader(queue)
    assert(reader:transferto(destination:newwriter()) == 7)
end
assert(destination:getstring() == "payload")
```

Neither takes the queue over. Nothing here frees or closes it, and one read to
empty leaves an empty queue rather than a released one.

::: seealso
- [](io.files) for the same contracts over a file on disk
- [](io) for filesystem names rather than file contents
- [ownership.md](docs/learn/runtime/ownership/borrowing.md) for the complete
  contract every owned value here follows
- [standard-library.md](docs/learn/runtime/data/standard-library.md) for the
  errors, ownership, and byte-position conventions every facility shares
:::
]]

-- Public names are direct aliases; calls reach the implementation without
-- adapters.
const bytes = require("nupp.io.internal.bytes")
const scalars = require("nupp.io.internal.scalars")

--- A forward-only byte source.
---
--- An interface rather than the concrete things that satisfy it. A buffer's
--- reader, an open file and an HTTP response body are all one of these, so
--- code written against the contract works over any of them without knowing
--- which it has.
--- @export
export type Reader = bytes.Reader

--- @internal
export const Reader = bytes.readerdefaults

--- An appending byte sink.
---
--- The counterpart of `Reader`, and an interface for the same reason: a buffer,
--- an open file and a request body all satisfy it, and a producer written
--- against the contract fills any of them.
--- @export
export type Writer = bytes.Writer

--- A queue of bytes something else is filling, which a reader may be opened
--- over without owning it.
---
--- A process stream and an HTTP response body are both one of these without
--- either knowing about this module.
--- @export
export type ByteQueue = bytes.ByteQueue

--- A snapshot of bytes that cannot be written through.
--- @export
export type ByteView = bytes.ByteView

--- An exclusive prepared region of a buffer. Its span may be written, then the
--- lease is committed with the number of bytes that actually landed. Dropping a
--- lease aborts it without changing the buffer's logical length.
--- @export
export type BufferWriteLease = bytes.BufferWriteLease

--- Growable byte storage whose pointer and logical length never escape
--- separately.
--- @export
export type Buffer = bytes.Buffer

--- Why an io operation failed: a kind to branch on and a message to show.
---
--- It prints as its message, and joins text as its message does, so code that
--- treats a reason as text keeps working. Compare `kind` rather than matching
--- the message, which is the platform's own words and differs between them.
--- @export
export record Error
    --- What kind of failure an io operation met, the same on every platform.
    ---
    --- - `notfound`: the file, program, host or other name does not exist.
    --- - `permission`: the platform refused the operation to this process.
    --- - `timeout`: a deadline passed before the operation finished.
    --- - `refused`: the peer turned the connection away.
    --- - `closed`: the resource, or the far end of it, has already gone.
    --- - `invalid`: what was sent or received was not well formed.
    --- - `limit`: a size, count or capacity bound was reached.
    --- - `unsupported`: this platform or provider cannot do it at all.
    --- - `other`: anything else; the message says what.
    type Kind = "notfound"
        | "permission"
        | "timeout"
        | "refused"
        | "closed"
        | "invalid"
        | "limit"
        | "unsupported"
        | "other"

    --- Routes a child's standard error to a pipe, its parent, nothing, or stdout.
    type Mode = "pipe" | "inherit" | "null" | "stdout"

    --- What kind of failure it was.
    kind: Kind

    --- What happened, in the platform's or the module's words.
    message: string

    metamethod __tostring: function(self): string

    metamethod __concat: function(left: string | number | Error, right: string | number | Error): string
end

Error.__tostring = function(self: Error): string
    return self.message
end

Error.__concat = function(left: string | number | Error, right: string | number | Error): string
    return tostring(left) .. tostring(right)
end

-- The platform's words for each kind, most specific first. Matched against the
-- lowercased message, so the native providers' operating-system text, the
-- browser's DOMException names and this library's own reasons all land on the
-- same kind. The order matters where one message could match two: a refused
-- connection that timed out is a timeout.
local KIND_PATTERNS: {
    {string}
} = {
    {"timeout", "timed out"},
    {"timeout", "timeouterror"},
    {"timeout", "deadline"},
    {"notfound", "no such file"},
    {"notfound", "not found"},
    {"notfound", "notfounderror"},
    {"notfound", "cannot find"},
    {"notfound", "does not exist"},
    {"notfound", "no such host"},
    {"notfound", "failed to lookup address"},
    {"notfound", "name or service not known"},
    {"notfound", "nodename nor servname"},
    {"refused", "connection refused"},
    {"refused", "actively refused"},
    {"refused", "connectionrefused"},
    {"permission", "permission denied"},
    {"permission", "access is denied"},
    {"permission", "not permitted"},
    {"permission", "notallowederror"},
    {"permission", "securityerror"},
    {"closed", "broken pipe"},
    {"closed", "connection reset"},
    {"closed", "connection aborted"},
    {"closed", "is closed"},
    {"closed", "was closed"},
    {"closed", "no longer reading"},
    {"closed", "aborterror"},
    {"limit", "exceed"},
    {"limit", "too large"},
    {"limit", "too many"},
    {"limit", "too long"},
    {"limit", "quotaexceedederror"},
    {"limit", "no space"},
    {"limit", "capacity"},
    {"unsupported", "not supported"},
    {"unsupported", "unsupported"},
    {"unsupported", "notsupportederror"},
    {"invalid", "invalid"},
    {"invalid", "malformed"},
    {"invalid", "typemismatcherror"},
}

--- Returns the kind a failure message describes, or `other`.
---
--- For providers, which mostly have only a platform's text to go on.
--- @param message the platform's or the provider's words
--- @return the kind those words describe
--- @internal
export function classify(message: string): Error.Kind
    const lowered = message:lower()
    for _, entry in ipairs(KIND_PATTERNS) do
        if lowered:find(entry[2], 1, true) ~= nil then
            return entry[1] as Error.Kind
        end
    end

    return "other"
end

--- Makes an error from a provider's reason: an error as it is, text classified
--- by `classify` unless a kind is given, and nothing as nothing.
--- @param reason the provider's reason, if it gave one
--- @param kind the kind to use instead of classifying text
--- @return the error, or nil when there was no reason
--- @internal
export function toError(reason: string | Error | nil, kind: Error.Kind?): Error?
    if reason == nil then
        return nil
    end
    if reason is Error then
        return reason
    end
    const message = tostring(reason)

    return new Error(kind = kind ?? classify(message), message = message)
end

--- Reads fixed-width numbers from a source of bytes.
--- @export
export type ScalarReader = scalars.ScalarReader

--- Writes fixed-width numbers into a destination.
--- @export
export type ScalarWriter = scalars.ScalarWriter

--- A reader taken apart into lines.
---
--- Reading a line means reading past its end, so something has to hold what
--- came after it. That is what this owns, and it is why a line reader takes its
--- source rather than borrowing one: two things reading the same bytes would
--- each see half of them.
--- @export
export type Lines = bytes.Lines

--- Creates a buffer, optionally over starting bytes or a starting capacity.
---
--- The buffer is an owner, so it closes at its lexical boundary unless it is
--- transferred out of one. A number is a capacity to allocate rather than
--- content, and the buffer is still empty afterwards.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local buffer = io.newbuffer("hello")
--- assert(buffer:len() == 5)
---
--- local sized = io.newbuffer(4096)
--- assert(sized:len() == 0 and sized:capacity() == 4096)
--- ```
---
--- @param initial the starting bytes, a starting capacity, or nothing
--- @return the buffer, owned by the caller
--- @raises string when initial is neither bytes nor a capacity
--- @export
export const newBuffer = bytes.newBuffer

--- Opens a reader over bytes held as a string.
---
--- The reader is a `Reader` like any other, so a parser written against the
--- contract reads a string here and a file or a response body elsewhere without
--- changing:
---
--- ```nupp
---
--- local reader = io.newreader("hello")
--- assert(reader:read(5) == "hello")
--- assert(reader:read(1) == "")
--- ```
---
--- @param text the bytes to read
--- @return the reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises string when text is not a string
--- @export
export const newStringReader = bytes.newStringReader

--- Opens a byte reader over a string, queue, buffer, or view.
export const newReader = bytes.newReader

--- Opens a byte writer that clears and exclusively borrows a buffer.
export const newWriter = bytes.newWriter

--- Opens a reader over a byte queue, which it does not own.
---
--- Closing this reader leaves the queue behind it open, because the queue was
--- never transferred. A process stream and an HTTP response body are both byte
--- queues without naming this module.
---
--- @param source the queue to read from
--- @return the reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises string when source is not a byte queue
--- @export
export const newQueueReader = bytes.newQueueReader

--- Opens a cursor for sized integer and float reads.
---
--- A string, snapshot or buffer is read from a copy of its bytes taken here,
--- and reports its `remaining` count. A reader or a queue is consumed in place:
--- the reader returns no count, the queue returns what it still holds.
---
--- What the source is decides what happens to it. A snapshot or a buffer is
--- borrowed and outlives the cursor; a reader is taken, and closing the cursor
--- closes it; a string or a queue carries no obligation either way.
---
--- ```nupp:fragment
--- local cursor = io.newsreader(header)
--- local magic = cursor:readuint32()
--- local count = cursor:readuint16()
--- ```
---
--- @param source the bytes, snapshot, buffer, reader or queue to read
--- @return the new reader, owned by the caller
--- @raises string when source is none of those
--- @export
export const newScalarReader = scalars.newScalarReader

--- Opens a cursor for sized integer and float writes.
---
--- A buffer is appended to, keeping what it already holds. A writer receives
--- the bytes as they are written. Omitting the destination starts a fresh
--- buffer, which `ScalarWriter:buffer` hands back.
---
--- ```nupp
--- local cursor = io.newswriter()
--- cursor:writeuint32(0x4d495355):writeuint16(2)
--- local bytes = cursor:buffer()
--- assert(bytes ~= nil and bytes:getstring() ~= "")
--- ```
---
--- @param destination the buffer or writer to append to, or nothing for a
---     fresh buffer
--- @return the new writer, owned by the caller
--- @raises string when destination is neither a buffer nor a writer
--- @export
export const newScalarWriter = scalars.newScalarWriter

--- Reads a reader to its end and returns everything it held as a string.
---
--- The reader is taken, and closed on every way out. A limit is checked as the
--- bytes arrive, so a source larger than it returns a reason rather than being
--- held whole.
--- @param reader the reader to drain
--- @param maxbytes the most bytes to accept, or no limit
--- @return the bytes, or nil when the reader failed or held more than the limit
--- @return why it could not, when unsuccessful
--- @raises string when maxbytes is not a positive integer
--- @export
export const readAll = bytes.readAll
